From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui: when a move over the VM gives the guest only the pointer's place, tested without AppKit

What: a header with no AppKit in it: the rule for a move over one of the
VM's windows while another app is in front. The guest gets the pointer's
place (and nothing else) when the guest's pointer is absolute, the VM does
not have the pointer, OmacVM is not the app in front, it is a move with no
button held, Touch ID's panel is down, the VM shows on two or more Mac
displays, the window is in full screen and nothing is above it under the
pointer; OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER=0 turns it off. Also how often the debug line
may come. Used by omacvm-cocoa-pointer-hover.patch (which explains the why).

Why its own file: so the rule is tested without QEMU or a window
(app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-hover.sh applies this patch to an
empty folder; at build time and in CI).

Upstream: with omacvm-cocoa-pointer-hover.patch; not sent.
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-hover.c.

--- a/ui/omacvm-pointer-hover.h
+++ b/ui/omacvm-pointer-hover.h
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+/*
+ * OmacVM: the pointer's place to the guest while another app is in front.
+ *
+ * No AppKit in here, so it is tested on its own
+ * (app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-hover.c); ui/cocoa.m uses it.
+ *
+ * In full screen on two or more displays one display can show macOS (the
+ * person switched its Space) with a Mac app in front, while another one
+ * still shows the VM. QEMU let go of the pointer when that app came to the
+ * front, and a move over a VM window took the pointer only while OmacVM was
+ * the app in front: a pointer just passing over the VM must not take the
+ * keyboard from the Mac app. So on the display that still showed the VM the
+ * guest's pointer stood still until a click: no hover, scrolls at the old
+ * place (2026-10-28). Now such a move gives the guest the pointer's place
+ * and nothing else. The keyboard stays with the Mac app, the Mac's cursor
+ * stays as it is (shown, as at boot), no window becomes key and nothing
+ * comes to the front. A click still takes the pointer as before.
+ */
+#ifndef OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER_H
+#define OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER_H
+
+#include <stdbool.h>
+#include <string.h>
+
+typedef struct OmacVMHoverIn {
+    bool off;       /* OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER=0: as before, such moves dropped */
+    bool absolute;  /* the guest's pointer is absolute (its tablet) */
+    bool grabbed;   /* the VM has the pointer: the normal path */
+    bool active;    /* OmacVM is the app in front: the normal path */
+    bool move;      /* a move, not a click */
+    bool buttons;   /* a mouse button is held (a drag that started in a Mac app) */
+    bool touchid;   /* Touch ID's panel is up: the VM gets no input then */
+    int displays;   /* Mac displays the VM shows an output on */
+    bool full;      /* the window is in full screen (another display's window always is) */
+    bool top;       /* the pointer is on that window, nothing above it */
+} OmacVMHoverIn;
+
+/*
+ * Only with two or more displays: on one display the VM shows nowhere else
+ * while another app is in front, and that case stays as it was.
+ */
+static inline bool omacvm_pointer_hover(const OmacVMHoverIn *in)
+{
+    return !in->off && in->absolute && !in->grabbed && !in->active && in->move &&
+           !in->buttons && !in->touchid && in->displays >= 2 && in->full && in->top;
+}
+
+/* OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER: "0" turns it off; unset or anything else: on. */
+static inline bool omacvm_pointer_hover_off(const char *env)
+{
+    return env && !strcmp(env, "0");
+}
+
+/* With OMACVM_POINTER_DEBUG=1: one line at most this often while it hovers. */
+#define OMACVM_HOVER_LOG_S 10.0
+
+/* last < 0: never logged. */
+static inline bool omacvm_hover_log_due(double now, double *last)
+{
+    if (*last >= 0 && now >= *last && now - *last < OMACVM_HOVER_LOG_S) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    *last = now;
+    return true;
+}
+
+#endif /* OMACVM_POINTER_HOVER_H */
